      "widgets_values": [
        "# Pulse Slate 3 · retake a bad three seconds\n\n**A different job with a different input.** Everything else in this pack starts\nfrom a timeline. This starts from a *finished film* — load one, name the span\nthat is wrong, and only that span re-renders.\n\n## What it actually does\n\n1. **The cut snaps to the frame grid.** H3 renders `17k+5` frames and nothing\n   else, so the patch length is snapped and *the cut moves with it*. Ask for\n   4.00 s → 4.20 s and the in/out points shift accordingly. The `plan` output\n   says exactly where they landed — read it.\n2. **The stitch preserves length.** The result is the same number of frames as\n   the base clip. A retake never shortens or lengthens the film, so anything cut\n   to it stays in sync.\n3. **Both surrounding frames are pinned.** The frame before the cut and the frame\n   after it are the patch's first and last frames. That is the fl2va branch\n   specifically — which is why this node takes `model_fl2va` and not the\n   reference checkpoint.\n\n## keep_base_audio is on, and should stay on\n\nA re-rendered patch invents its own score. It will not match the track either\nside of it, and a three-second audio mismatch in the middle of a scene is far more\nobvious than a three-second picture mismatch. Leave this on unless the audio is\nthe thing you are fixing.\n\n## Set the span from the plan, not from the timeline\n\n`cut_start_seconds` / `cut_end_seconds` are in seconds on the **base clip**, from\nits own frame 0. Scrub the loaded video, take the numbers, queue with `dry_run`\noff — the `plan` output tells you what it snapped to before anything renders.\n"
      ],
      "title": "READ ME"
    }
